{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 560,
    "dDurationMs": 7630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Neils Bohr said, “It is wrong to think that the \ntask of physics is to find out how Nature is.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8190,
    "dDurationMs": 6450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Physics concerns what we can say about Nature.” \nWell it turns out that if we pay attention to this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14640,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "subtle difference, some of the most mysterious \naspects of nature make a lot more sense."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25760,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What is physics really trying to do? Is it to find \nthe mathematical laws that govern the universe?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 32640,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Not quite - no one has to solve the Schrodinger \nequation in order for an electron to be able to do  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39120,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its thing. Our laws of nature are just models. So \nmaybe the job of physics is one step removed - to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46000,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "come up with laws that do the best job at \npredicting how the universe works, and then hope  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51600,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that we can infer truths about the universe based \non which laws work best. But actually, some of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57520,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "founders of quantum theory were convinced that \nthe role of physics was one step further removed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62560,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "still. Neils Bohr insisted that what we actually \nmodel is the results of observation, not the world  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69040,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "itself. His student and closest colleague, Werner \nHeisenberg, put it well “The laws of nature which  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76160,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we formulate mathematically in quantum theory deal \nno longer with the particles themselves but with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81920,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our knowledge of the elementary particles.” \nIn other words, the mathematical laws of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86960,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physics don’t govern reality, they’re not even \ndirect models of reality. Rather, the laws of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92960,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physics are models of our experience of reality. \nPhysics models our information about the world."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101040,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In a recent episode we started talking \nabout informational interpretations  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105600,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of quantum mechanics. Then we discussed \none of the more radical interpretations:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110480,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "John Archibald Wheeler’s idea that information \nis really the most fundamental thing, and it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116240,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gives rise to the physical - a notion he pithily \nsummarized with the expression “it from bit”.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123440,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Quantum mechanics tells us that asking questions \nof the universe radically changes how it behaves.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129280,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Wheeler followed that simple fact down the \nrabbit hole to what he saw as the logical  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133200,
    "dDurationMs": 6830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "conclusion - the most fundamental existence is between the relationship between the observer and the observed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140030,
    "dDurationMs": 7002,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Although he was careful to note that observer in this context didn't necessarily need to be conscious. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147032,
    "dDurationMs": 4008,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Wheeler may have been right, but we \ncan explore the power of informational  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151040,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum mechanics without committing \nto quite such a radical interpretation.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155360,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we’re going to see how a lot \nof the weirdness of quantum mechanics  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158880,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can make sense if we think about it as a \nmodel of our information about the world."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164400,
    "dDurationMs": 5519,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Normally in physics we try to break up the \nworld into its most elementary components.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 169919,
    "dDurationMs": 6321,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We learn how those components behave, and see \nif we can rebuild the world from those parts.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176240,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In quantum mechanics, we have things like \nparticles and fields which can only take on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 180880,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "discrete or quantized values. These quantum \ncomponents also have weird properties like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186720,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fundamental uncertainty in their values \nand strange correlations between components  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191920,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that we call entanglement. But as weird \nas quantum fields and particles are,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197360,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this still feels like a very physical way \nto define the building blocks of reality."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202560,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The physicist Anton Zeilinger has proposed an \ninformational approach to quantum mechanics  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208240,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in which the world is broken up not into \nphysical parts, but into informational parts.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214160,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what does it mean to use information as \nour building block? Information represents  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218880,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our knowledge about the world. So our new \nbuilding block becomes a statement about  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224080,
    "dDurationMs": 5988,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the information we have — for example, about \nthe location or speed or mass of a particle.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230068,
    "dDurationMs": 5212,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Zeilinger calls such a statement a proposition \n- it’s an answer to a question we could ask  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235280,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about the world. He says that a quantum \nsystem is a collection of propositions.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241040,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A quantum system represents our knowledge \nof the world, not the world itself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245600,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So how do you break up a “world” made of knowledge \nor information? Well, an informational building  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 251600,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "block is the answer to a question. So the smallest \ninformational building block is the answer to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256800,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a question with the fewest possible outcomes. \nLiterally any answer can be reached by a series  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263840,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of well-chosen yes-no questions. So Zeilinger \nsays that any quantum system can be broken  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270960,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into the results of binary questions. By insisting \nthat the most elementary informational building  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277120,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "block contains only one bit - one yes-no answer \n- a surprising number of the weird results of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283920,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum mechanics suddenly make sense. Things \nlike quantum indeterminacy, entanglement, and the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290560,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "uncertainty principle turn out to be the expected \nbehaviors of this sort of information system."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296960,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The simplest way to start is to look at a \nquantum system where the answer to a single  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301760,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "binary question seems to give a meaningful \n“physical” answer. Consider quantum spin.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 308640,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "From a physical point of view, think of it as \na particle’s orientation - a spin axis that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 314880,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can point either up or down. But really the \nquantum system is the answer to a question:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320800,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is the spin up or down relative to the direction \nof measurement? We ask this question with a Stern  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 328560,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gerlach apparatus, where the magnetic moment \nof the particles interact with a magnetic field  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333680,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gradient to deflect the particles either up or \ndown, depending on the direction of the spin."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 338960,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s say we prepare an electron’s spin \nto all point up relative to our apparatus.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 344000,
    "dDurationMs": 6961,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The spin contains one bit of information, one possible answer when we ask the electron what direction is your spin?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350961,
    "dDurationMs": 2479,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That answer is up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353440,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what if we asked the \nelectron spin a different question “are you pointing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358720,
    "dDurationMs": 6604,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "left or right?” We can do this by rotating \nthe Stern Gerlach apparatus 90 degrees.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365324,
    "dDurationMs": 4756,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You started out with one bit of knowledge \nabout the particle’s up-down alignment.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370080,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "According to Zeilinger, by definition, that’s \nall the information that the elementary quantum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 375600,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "system of spin can contain. That means the \nleft-right orientation is undefined. It’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 382640,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in a superposition state of both left and right \nuntil it’s measured. Run it through  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 387200,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our horizontally aligned Stern-Gerlach \ndivide and the electron has an even chance  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 393040,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of being deflected left or right - its undefined \nleft-right spin chooses randomly between the two  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399760,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because it contains no information about its spin \nin that direction. And following that measurement,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 405360,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the left-right alignment of the spin has become \ndefined - after all, the electron has to come  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 410720,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up with an answer to the question you asked. \nBut now with the left-right alignment defined,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416320,
    "dDurationMs": 8240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our single bit of spin information is used up. The up-down alignment becomes undefined. So  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424560,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by thinking of quantum systems as being made \nof these elementary “quanta of information”,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430400,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we see the indeterminacy of quantum theory arises \nnaturally. Zeilinger even managed to derive the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437440,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equivalent of the Schrodinger equation by asking \nhow quantum information should evolve over time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 443600,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Quantum entanglement also fits this picture. \nWhen we prepared our electrons to be spin-up,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 450000,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that spin was relative to a chosen direction - the \nvertical in this case. But we could also prepare  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 455920,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an electron to have a spin direction that’s \ndefined relative to another particle’s spin.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 461120,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, a pair of electrons could be \nprepared that have opposite spin to each other.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 466880,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now remember our requirement that the spin of each \nelectron can only contain one bit of information,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 472640,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and that bit is taken up to describe its \nrelationship to its partner electron.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478320,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now the information is no longer isolated in the \nsingle electron’s spin, but rather spread between  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484880,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "two electrons. Two electron spins contain two \nbits, but those bits are distributed non-locally.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492960,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you ask the spin direction of either of \nthose electrons relative to your Stern-Gerlach  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 498240,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "apparatus, you’ll learn the spin direction of \nboth particles. And in making that measurement,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 504400,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you’ve forced the single bit of spin information \nin both electrons to become defined relative to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 510560,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your apparatus - whichever way you chose to \nalign it. Our distributed information becomes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516880,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "local to each electron, and in the process \nappears to force an instantaneous communication  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522720,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "between the particles - a spooky action \nat a distance. This is entanglement."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529280,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So we’ve seen how an elementary quantum system’s \ninformation content has to exist with respect to a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 536560,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "certain type of question that you ask of it. Spin \ndirection in the last example. This idea leads us  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 542400,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "naturally to some other staples of quantum theory \n- like the Heisenberg uncertainty principle.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549040,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In a previous episode we saw how the uncertainty \nprinciple arises from the limited knowledge that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 554400,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we can extract from a quantum wavefunction. For \nexample, that the product of the measurement error  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560240,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in a particle’s position and momentum has to be \ngreater than the Planck constant divided by 4-pi."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 566240,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, by bringing to bear \nthe tools of information theory  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 570400,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we can derive an even tighter uncertainty relation \n- something known as entropic uncertainty.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 577440,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This approach uses the informational definition \nof entropy - Shannon entropy - which is a measure  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 584720,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the number of yes-no questions needed to \nextract all the information from a system.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 590560,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The resulting “entropic uncertainty” \nhas been used to explain one of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 595680,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "original and most mysterious features of \nquantum mechanics - wave-particle duality."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 602080,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This was demonstrated in by \na team of physicists in 2014.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 606960,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They applied entropic uncertainty to analyze \nWheeler’s “delayed-choice” experiment. This  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 612880,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "experiment causes a photon to behave like a wave \nor a particle depending on the question asked  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 620160,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of it. And that question could be asked after \nit passes through the experimental apparatus.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 626720,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If it’s a wave it travels both paths of the \ndevice, if a particle it must only travel one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 633680,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Applying the notion of entropic \nuncertainty to the situation,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 637600,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the team said that the wavefunction contained \nonly one answer to two complementary questions:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 644640,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just as our particle could only tell you if it was up or down, but not left or right,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 652160,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the wavefunction of the photon they considered \ncould tell you either which path the photon took,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 659040,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or the phase of the photon by looking \nat the interference pattern.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 663600,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because of the finite information content \nof the wavefunction, it didn’t have answers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668080,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to both questions. So, they found that the \nwave-particle duality of quantum mechanics  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 673680,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "arises from the limited information, the \ninability to answer two complementary questions:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680400,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“Are you a particle?” And “are \nyou a wave?” at the same time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684720,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s one thing to use quantum information theory \nas a mathematical tool, but quite another to claim  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690160,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that information is somehow a more fundamental than the tangible stuff that most people  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696080,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "believe to be the building blocks of reality. In \nquantum mechanics, we tend to think of the quantum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 700720,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wavefunction as pretty fundamental. It describes \nthe evolving distribution of probabilities for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 707200,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the results you might get if you tried to \nmeasure the properties of a quantum system.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 712400,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Zeilinger and supporters would say that the wavefunction does not have a physical  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 717920,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "existence independent of the observer. Rather, \nthe wavefunction and the math that governs it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725520,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "describe our information about the \nuniverse, not the universe itself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 729840,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But, if the wavefunction is about \nthe information content of a system,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 734080,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "again, we come back to the same question \nthat plagued Wheeler: “whose information?”  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740320,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This same question haunted Einstein. Einstein \nfamously once asked whether proponents of these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 746960,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "observer-centric interpretations truly believe \nthe moon isn’t there when nobody looks. To this,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 754400,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bohr and Wheeler and Zeilinger and their \nsupporters might say, “You can’t prove that it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 760080,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is.” To learn about something necessarily involves \nan observer who is acquiring this knowledge,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 766320,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so all we can ever know about the world is \nhow we interact with it. Whether there is an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772640,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "observer-independent world out there, we, being \nobservers, can never prove. And it turns out that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 780480,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a lot of the weirdness we see in the quantum world \nmake more sense if we pay attention to the fact  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 786240,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that our only direct experience is with \nan entirely informational space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 793760,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Look, the universe almost certainly is not a simulation. But you can’t be too careful. If you agree and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 800160,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would like to spread the word, or if you just \nfind this t-shirt amusing, you can pick one up  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 805040,
    "dDurationMs": 5964,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at pbsspacetime.com, along with the rest \nof our merch. Link in the description."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 811004,
    "dDurationMs": 4516,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, In the last episode we talked about the \nformation history of the Milky Way, told through  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 815520,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the various acts of galactic cannibalism performed \nby our galaxy. Let’s see what you had to say."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 821680,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Martijn van Oosterhout asks what happens to \nthe central black holes of galaxies that the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 826480,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "milky way eats. The answer is simple - the Milky \nWay’s central black hole eats those black holes.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 832960,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Eventually, anyway. If the swallowed galaxy is \nbig enough to have its own so-called supermassive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 838800,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole, that black hole will end up in some \norbit within our galaxy’s gravitational well.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 844800,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’ll interact with stars, kicking them up to \nhigher orbits while its own orbit decays. In this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 850720,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "way it’ll spiral towards the center and eventually \nmerge with our own supermassive black hole,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 856000,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "making it supermassiver. This is one of the \nmain ways that supermassive black holes grow."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 862400,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Erik Ziak asks how galaxy interactions \naffect the galactic habitable zone,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 867440,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "suggesting that it may be less of a \nzone and rather a more complex region.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 872160,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Erik is referring to the recent episode where \nwe talked about what parts of the Milky Way  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 876400,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could potentially host life based on various \nfactors like abundance of necessary elements  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 882400,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and there not being too many supernovae. The \ngalactic habitable zone is defined to be a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 887760,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ring around the disk, not too close to the core \nand not too far out. It may be that there are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894480,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "better and worse parts of this ring, but remember \nthat a star will move through many different  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 900080,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "regions as it orbits the galaxy. It’ll be born in \na spiral arm probably, and then move in and out of spiral  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 908000,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "arms every hundred million years or so. It’ll dip \nabove and below the plane of the disk slightly.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 914240,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Life on Earth has persisted for billions of years, \nso it’s survived various regions - although it may  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 919600,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be that mass extinction events correlate with \nentering more dangerous regions of the galaxy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 925680,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "David Kosa asks about actual collisions between \nstars when galaxies merge. Given that most of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 932800,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "galaxy is empty space, what’s the probability \nof stars colliding? Well for any given star  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 938880,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the chance is very small - given that stars \nare of order a light-second across, while the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 945120,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distance between them is light years. I’ve heard \nan estimate for the number of stellar collisions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 950320,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that are expected to occur when the Milky Way and \nAndromeda collide - out of the trillion-ish stars  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 956720,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "involved in the merger, expect approximately one \ncollision. And what happens to the stars in that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 964240,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collision? It depends on how head-on the collision \nis, and the mass ratio between the stars.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 970080,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Glancing collisions can result in stars merging, \nwhich increases the mass and also rejuvenates the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 975840,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "star because more hydrogen fuel gets pumped into \nthe fusion core. It’s believed that the oddly blue  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 983040,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stars at the cores of globular clusters may have \nresulted from stellar mergers in these hyper-dense  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 989360,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bundles of stars. On the other hand, head-on \ncollisions tend to disrupt stars. For example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 995760,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its been shown that a more compact star punching \nthrough a more diffuse star will accelerate fusion  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1001760,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the core of the latter, causing it to blow \nitself to pieces. I know of at least one case  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1007440,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where this is believed to have happened due \nto a black hole or neutron star impactor."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1012640,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At the end of the last comment responses I \nmisinterpreted a couple of comments that said that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1018000,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the expansion of the universe could be thought \nof as matter shrinking rather than the universe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1022880,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expanding. I took those comments as being humourous - but \nprobably I was projecting because I sometimes feel  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1031040,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the temptation to give very plausible \nnonsense when someone asks for an explanation.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1037600,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At the american museum of natural history for example there’s \na mammoth skeleton and right next to it a tiny  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1044240,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bronze model of what the animal is thought to have \nlooked like. I’ve convinced quite a few people  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1050000,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the model represents the pygmy mammoth that \nlived at the same time as its gigantic cousin.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1055680,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I always correct the misinformation afterwards, \nbut it’s hard to resist the joke. Sorry if I  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1061120,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "misinterpreted the comments. It is actually \nan interesting question: why can’t matter be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1067360,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shrinking instead of the universe expanding? In \nterms of the relative scale of distances and sizes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1074240,
    "dDurationMs": 9040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the two are identical. However a number of things \nwould have to change in concert for  matter to be shrinking -  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1083280,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in particular the strength of the various forces. \nYet to expand space you just need to change space,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1091280,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and general relativity predicts that this should \nhappen. Also, we wouldn’t expect cosmological  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1096720,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "redshift if matter is shrinking because there’s no \nstretching of the traveling photons. And finally,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1102400,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in a universe where galaxies shrink you’d expect \npygmy mammoths, which I’ve already told you don’t  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1107920,
    "dDurationMs": 6332,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exist. Instead we see the gigantic mammoths the type predicted by the expanding universe model."
    } ]
  } ]
}
